      "side": 1,
      "message": "Often the suggestions don\u0027t work verbatim, but the *idea* is good. In that case, it\u0027s good to explore what it would take to implement. For example, the idea that is suggested here is that we should check to see if we logged the error message to make sure we actually hit the error case.\n\nFor example, some further exploration in Gemini provides the following. I think it\u0027s more complex than we want for this test, but it\u0027s always good to check because often Zephyr has built-in functions to do this type of stuff already.\n\n\n----\n\n# Guide: Asserting Specific Log Messages in Zephyr Ztest\n\nBy default, Zephyr routes `LOG_ERR()`, `LOG_INF()`, etc., directly to the console. To verify that a specific log message was triggered during a test, we cannot use a standard Ztest macro. Instead, we must create a **temporary custom log backend** within our test file to intercept the logs, format them, and assert on their contents.\n\nHere is how to set it up.\n\n## Step 1: Configure Kconfig\n\nFor unit testing logs, you want to process logs synchronously. If logs are deferred to a background thread, your test might finish and assert before the log is actually processed.\n\nAdd the following to your test\u0027s `prj.conf` (or `test.yaml`):\n\n```ini\nCONFIG_ZTEST\u003dy\nCONFIG_LOG\u003dy\n# Process logs immediately to avoid async thread timing issues\nCONFIG_LOG_MODE_IMMEDIATE\u003dy \n\n```\n\n## Step 2: The Implementation Boilerplate\n\nDrop this boilerplate into your `test_*.c` file. This code creates a custom log backend, intercepts the raw log message, formats it into a standard C string, and checks it against an expected string.\n\n```c\n#include \u003czephyr/ztest.h\u003e\n#include \u003czephyr/logging/log.h\u003e\n#include \u003czephyr/logging/log_backend.h\u003e\n#include \u003czephyr/logging/log_output.h\u003e\n#include \u003cstring.h\u003e\n\n/* Register this file with the logging subsystem */\nLOG_MODULE_REGISTER(my_test_module);\n\n/* --- Test State Variables --- */\nstatic bool specific_error_found \u003d false;\nstatic const char *expected_error_string \u003d NULL;\n\n/* --- Capture Buffers --- */\nstatic uint8_t capture_buf[256];\nstatic size_t capture_len \u003d 0;\nstatic uint8_t format_workspace[128];\n\n/* \n * 1. Output Callback\n * Zephyr\u0027s formatter calls this to dump characters. We route them into our buffer.\n */\nstatic int capture_output_func(uint8_t *data, size_t length, void *ctx)\n{\n    for (size_t i \u003d 0; i \u003c length; i++) {\n        if (capture_len \u003c sizeof(capture_buf) - 1) {\n            capture_buf[capture_len++] \u003d data[i];\n        }\n    }\n    capture_buf[capture_len] \u003d \u0027\\0\u0027; /* Ensure null-termination */\n    return length;\n}\n\n/* Tie the output function to a log_output object */\nLOG_OUTPUT_DEFINE(capture_log_output, capture_output_func, \n                  format_workspace, sizeof(format_workspace));\n\n/* \n * 2. Backend Processor Callback\n * This triggers on every log message system-wide.\n */\nstatic void test_backend_process(const struct log_backend *const backend,\n                                 union log_msg *msg)\n{\n    /* Filter for ERROR level logs (Change to LOG_LEVEL_INF/WRN if needed) */\n    if (log_msg_get_level(msg) \u003d\u003d LOG_LEVEL_ERR) {\n        \n        /* Reset capture buffer */\n        capture_len \u003d 0;\n        capture_buf[0] \u003d \u0027\\0\u0027;\n\n        /* Format the log message into our capture_buf (0 \u003d no ANSI colors) */\n        log_output_msg_process(\u0026capture_log_output, msg, 0);\n\n        /* Check if our expected string exists in the formatted output */\n        if (expected_error_string !\u003d NULL) {\n            if (strstr((char *)capture_buf, expected_error_string) !\u003d NULL) {\n                specific_error_found \u003d true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/* 3. Register the mock backend */\nstatic const struct log_backend_api test_backend_api \u003d {\n    .process \u003d test_backend_process,\n};\nLOG_BACKEND_DEFINE(test_log_backend, test_backend_api, true);\n\n```\n\n## Step 3: Write the Ztest Cases\n\nWith the boilerplate in place, checking for log messages is incredibly simple. Just set the `expected_error_string`, run your code, and assert on the `specific_error_found` flag.\n\n```c\n/* --- The Code Under Test --- */\nvoid initialize_sensor(int sensor_id)\n{\n    if (sensor_id \u003c 0) {\n        LOG_ERR(\"Failed to initialize sensor %d due to invalid ID\", sensor_id);\n        return;\n    }\n}\n\n/* --- The Test Cases --- */\nZTEST(my_test_suite, test_sensor_init_invalid_id_logs_error)\n{\n    /* Setup: Reset the flag and define what we expect to see */\n    specific_error_found \u003d false;\n    expected_error_string \u003d \"Failed to initialize sensor -5\";\n\n    /* Execution: Call the function that should fail and log */\n    initialize_sensor(-5);\n\n    /* Assertion: Did the backend catch and match our string? */\n    zassert_true(specific_error_found, \n                 \"Expected error log for sensor -5 was not printed!\");\n}\n\n```\n\n## How It Works (Important Notes)\n\n1. **Raw Messages vs. Formatted:** Zephyr log backends receive an unformatted `union log_msg *`. We cannot just read the string out of it because it might have arguments (like `%d`). We use `log_output_msg_process` to do the heavy lifting of `printf`-style formatting before we check the text.\n2. **Why we use `strstr`:** When Zephyr formats logs, it prepends metadata like `\u003cerr\u003e my_test_module: ...`. By using `strstr()` (string-search) instead of `strcmp()` (exact match), we can search for our specific payload and ignore the Zephyr-injected prefixes.\n3. **Resetting State:** Because test suites run sequentially in the same memory space, always ensure you reset `specific_error_found \u003d false;` and `expected_error_string \u003d NULL;` at the start of every test case (or in your test suite\u0027s `before` hook).",
